Task 1: Create a Snapshot of a Running VM
Subtask 1.1: Verify VM Status and Prepare Environment
First, let's check the status of available virtual machines and ensure our target VM is running.

# List all virtual machines
sudo virsh list --all

# Check the status of our target VM (assuming VM name is 'centos8-vm')
sudo virsh domstate centos8-vm

# If the VM is not running, start it
sudo virsh start centos8-vm

# Verify the VM is running
sudo virsh list --state-running
Subtask 1.2: Examine Current VM Configuration
Before creating a snapshot, it's important to understand the current VM configuration.

# Display detailed VM information
sudo virsh dominfo centos8-vm

# Show VM disk information
sudo virsh domblklist centos8-vm

# Check current snapshots (should be empty initially)
sudo virsh snapshot-list centos8-vm
Subtask 1.3: Create Initial Data for Testing
Let's create some test data in the VM that we can use to verify snapshot functionality.

# Connect to the VM console
sudo virsh console centos8-vm

# Once connected to the VM, create test files
echo "This is test data before snapshot" > /tmp/before_snapshot.txt
echo "Current date: $(date)" >> /tmp/before_snapshot.txt

# Create a test directory with multiple files
mkdir -p /tmp/test_data
for i in {1..5}; do
    echo "Test file $i created at $(date)" > /tmp/test_data/file$i.txt
done

# List the created files
ls -la /tmp/test_data/
cat /tmp/before_snapshot.txt

# Exit the VM console (Ctrl+] to disconnect)
Subtask 1.4: Create the VM Snapshot
Now we'll create our first snapshot of the running VM.

# Create a snapshot with descriptive name and description
sudo virsh snapshot-create-as centos8-vm \
    --name "baseline-snapshot" \
    --description "Initial snapshot with test data created on $(date)" \
    --disk-only

# Alternative: Create a full system snapshot (includes memory state)
sudo virsh snapshot-create-as centos8-vm \
    --name "full-baseline-snapshot" \
    --description "Full system snapshot with memory state on $(date)"

# Verify the snapshot was created successfully
sudo virsh snapshot-list centos8-vm

# Get detailed information about the snapshot
sudo virsh snapshot-info centos8-vm baseline-snapshot
Subtask 1.5: Verify Snapshot Creation
Let's verify that our snapshot was created properly and examine its properties.

# Display snapshot details in XML format
sudo virsh snapshot-dumpxml centos8-vm baseline-snapshot

# Check disk usage after snapshot creation
sudo du -sh /var/lib/libvirt/images/

# List all snapshots with detailed information
sudo virsh snapshot-list centos8-vm --tree
Task 2: Restore the VM from a Snapshot
Subtask 2.1: Modify VM State After Snapshot
Before restoring, let's make some changes to the VM to demonstrate the restore functionality.

# Connect to the VM again
sudo virsh console centos8-vm

# Make changes to test the restore process
echo "This data was added AFTER the snapshot" > /tmp/after_snapshot.txt
rm /tmp/test_data/file3.txt
echo "Modified content" >> /tmp/test_data/file1.txt

# Install a package to show system-level changes
sudo yum install -y htop

# Create additional test data
mkdir -p /tmp/post_snapshot_data
echo "This should disappear after restore" > /tmp/post_snapshot_data/temp.txt

# List current state
ls -la /tmp/
ls -la /tmp/test_data/
cat /tmp/after_snapshot.txt

# Exit VM console
Subtask 2.2: Prepare for Snapshot Restoration
Before restoring, we need to properly shut down the VM to ensure data consistency.

# Gracefully shutdown the VM
sudo virsh shutdown centos8-vm

# Wait for shutdown to complete
sleep 30

# Verify VM is shut down
sudo virsh domstate centos8-vm

# If VM doesn't shut down gracefully, force it
# sudo virsh destroy centos8-vm
Subtask 2.3: Restore VM from Snapshot
Now we'll restore the VM to its previous state using our snapshot.

# List available snapshots before restoration
sudo virsh snapshot-list centos8-vm

# Restore the VM to the baseline snapshot
sudo virsh snapshot-revert centos8-vm baseline-snapshot

# Verify the restoration was successful
sudo virsh domstate centos8-vm

# Start the VM if it's not running
sudo virsh start centos8-vm

# Wait for VM to boot
sleep 45
Subtask 2.4: Alternative Restoration Methods
Let's explore different restoration options available with virsh.

# Restore and automatically start the VM
sudo virsh snapshot-revert centos8-vm baseline-snapshot --running

# Restore with force option (if needed)
# sudo virsh snapshot-revert centos8-vm baseline-snapshot --force

# Restore to a paused state
# sudo virsh snapshot-revert centos8-vm baseline-snapshot --paused
Task 3: Verify the Integrity of the Restored VM
Subtask 3.1: Check File System Integrity
Let's verify that our VM has been properly restored to its snapshot state.

# Connect to the restored VM
sudo virsh console centos8-vm

# Check if our original test data is present
cat /tmp/before_snapshot.txt
ls -la /tmp/test_data/

# Verify that post-snapshot changes are gone
ls -la /tmp/after_snapshot.txt  # This should not exist
ls -la /tmp/post_snapshot_data/  # This directory should not exist

# Check that all original test files are present
for i in {1..5}; do
    if [ -f "/tmp/test_data/file$i.txt" ]; then
        echo "File $i exists: $(cat /tmp/test_data/file$i.txt)"
    else
        echo "File $i is missing!"
    fi
done
Subtask 3.2: Verify System State
Check that system-level changes made after the snapshot have been reverted.

# Check if htop package is still installed (it shouldn't be)
which htop
rpm -q htop

# Verify system logs for any errors during restoration
sudo journalctl --since "10 minutes ago" | grep -i error

# Check disk usage and file system integrity
df -h
sudo fsck -n /dev/vda1  # Replace with appropriate device
Subtask 3.3: Network and Service Verification
Ensure that network connectivity and services are functioning properly after restoration.

# Test network connectivity
ping -c 3 8.8.8.8

# Check system services status
sudo systemctl status sshd
sudo systemctl status NetworkManager

# Verify system time and date
date
timedatectl status

# Exit VM console
Subtask 3.4: Advanced Integrity Checks
Perform additional checks to ensure complete system integrity.

# From the host system, check VM configuration
sudo virsh dumpxml centos8-vm | grep -A 5 -B 5 disk

# Verify snapshot chain integrity
sudo virsh snapshot-list centos8-vm --tree

# Check for any snapshot-related errors in logs
sudo journalctl -u libvirtd | grep -i snapshot | tail -10

# Verify VM performance after restoration
sudo virsh domstats centos8-vm
Snapshot Management Best Practices
Creating Multiple Snapshots
# Create additional snapshots for different states
sudo virsh snapshot-create-as centos8-vm \
    --name "post-restore-verification" \
    --description "Snapshot after successful restoration verification"

# Create snapshots with specific disk targets
sudo virsh snapshot-create-as centos8-vm \
    --name "disk-only-snapshot" \
    --description "Disk-only snapshot for quick backup" \
    --disk-only \
    --diskspec vda,snapshot=external

# List all snapshots
sudo virsh snapshot-list centos8-vm --tree
Snapshot Cleanup and Management
# Delete old snapshots (be careful!)
# sudo virsh snapshot-delete centos8-vm old-snapshot-name

# Get current snapshot information
sudo virsh snapshot-current centos8-vm

# Create a snapshot management script
cat > snapshot_manager.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
VM_NAME="centos8-vm"
SNAPSHOT_NAME="auto-snapshot-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"

echo "Creating snapshot: $SNAPSHOT_NAME"
sudo virsh snapshot-create-as $VM_NAME \
    --name "$SNAPSHOT_NAME" \
    --description "Automated snapshot created on $(date)"

echo "Current snapshots for $VM_NAME:"
sudo virsh snapshot-list $VM_NAME
EOF

chmod +x snapshot_manager.sh
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Snapshot Creation Fails
# Check available disk space
df -h /var/lib/libvirt/images/

# Verify VM is in a consistent state
sudo virsh domstate centos8-vm

# Check for conflicting operations
sudo virsh list --all
ps aux | grep qemu
Issue 2: Restoration Problems
# Force stop VM if needed
sudo virsh destroy centos8-vm

# Check snapshot integrity
sudo virsh snapshot-list centos8-vm
sudo qemu-img check /var/lib/libvirt/images/centos8-vm.qcow2

# Restore with force option
sudo virsh snapshot-revert centos8-vm baseline-snapshot --force
Issue 3: Performance Issues After Restoration
# Check VM resource allocation
sudo virsh dominfo centos8-vm

# Monitor VM performance
sudo virsh domstats centos8-vm

# Restart libvirt services if needed
sudo systemctl restart libvirtd
Security Considerations
Snapshot Security Best Practices
# Set appropriate permissions on snapshot files
sudo find /var/lib/libvirt/images/ -name "*.qcow2" -exec chmod 600 {} \;
sudo chown qemu:qemu /var/lib/libvirt/images/*.qcow2

